n use to infer information about things that are quiet.\n11. **All blue things are young.** This tells us that all blue things belong to the category of young things.\n12. **Big, blue things are quiet.** This tells us that big blue things belong to the category of quiet things.\n13. **Young, white things are round.** This tells us that young white things belong to the category of round things.\n14. **If Fiona is big and Fiona is not white, then Fiona is blue.** This is a conditional statement that we can use to infer information about Fiona.\n15. **If Charlie is big and Charlie is young, then Charlie is not smart.** This is a conditional statement that we can use to infer information about Charlie.\n16. **Round things are big.** This tells us that round things belong to the category of big things.\n\nNow, let\'s analyze the statement "Gary is not round":.\n\n- From point 5, we know that Gary is blue.\n- From point 6, we know that Gary is round.\n- From point 11, we know that big blue things are quiet.\n- From point 12, we know that big blue things are quiet.\n- From point 13, we know that young white things are round.\n- From point 14, we know that if Fiona is big and Fiona is not white, then Fiona is blue.\n- From point 15, we know that if Charlie is big and Charlie is young, then Charlie is not smart.\n- From point 16, we know that round things are big.\n\nSince Gary is blue and round, and we have no information that contradicts this, we can conclude that Gary is indeed round.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Gary is not round" is false.\n\n<｜User｜>Please solve the logical reasoning problem. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
